{
 "version": 1,
 "_comment": [
  "Two SSS profiles in one frame, small enough that the silhouette is a real fraction",
  "of every sphere (spec 11.37 phase 3).",
  "",
  "The profile a pixel belongs to is stored as a LABEL NUMBER in attachment 4's alpha --",
  "0 = not skin, 1 = the first profile, 2 = the second -- and that attachment is",
  "MSAA-resolved by a blit, which is a box filter. Averaging a label destroys it: the mean",
  "of 2 and the uncovered 0 is 1, which does not mean 'half of profile 2', it means",
  "PROFILE 1. So at 4x MSAA the partly covered pixels of the tag-2 sphere are seeded into",
  "the tag-1 walk and blurred with the wrong profile's radius and colour.",
  "",
  "Only ONE of the two spheres can show that, and which one is not arbitrary:",
  "  sss_skin_a -> slot 0 -> tag 1. Its silhouette averages toward 0, which reads as 'not",
  "               skin', so those pixels are DROPPED. Absence, not corruption. The control.",
  "  sss_skin_b -> slot 1 -> tag 2. Its silhouette averages toward 1, so those pixels are",
  "               blurred as profile a. The subject.",
  "Slots are assigned in the order the materials appear in THIS BLOCK (configure_sss_",
  "materials, apps/render/src/render.c), and the slot is printed at startup, so the",
  "assignment is observable rather than inferred.",
  "",
  "The two profiles differ in CHANNEL WEIGHTS and share one radius, and that split was",
  "measured rather than guessed. Weights give the larger signal: opposite weights at equal",
  "radius move the read 3.6%, while equal weights at radii 1.2 against 0.35 move it only",
  "2.0%. The misfile substitutes the other profile's weights, and the scatter delta is what",
  "the blur differs from the sharp value by, so a weight difference acts on it directly.",
  "Equal radii also keep the halos from crossing the 26 px gap between the spheres, which a",
  "wide profile a does -- and since the twin the arm compares against has no profile a at",
  "all, a halo that reaches the read band would put a difference there that is nothing to do",
  "with the tag.",
  "",
  "The colour is a per-channel scatter WIDTH, not a tint (cscene.h), which is why these read",
  "as bleeds rather than as paint.",
  "",
  "The key is SIDE-ON, giving each sphere a terminator, and that is not a lighting",
  "preference -- it is what makes the measurement exist at all. The scatter delta is",
  "blur minus centre, so a uniformly lit sphere has blur == centre and contributes",
  "nothing however wrong its profile is. A back key was tried first and produced flat",
  "discs: the whole gather moved 129 px of the frame, the misfile moved 23, and the",
  "signal that looked like a 3.6% effect was BLOOM spreading the other sphere's shading",
  "across the read band. The tag chooses which profile blurs a pixel; where no profile",
  "blurs, choosing wrong is free. Same direction as skin_curvature_fixture's key, which",
  "is the fixture that does show scatter.",
  "",
  "Deliberately NOT a golden. It exists to differ between two sample counts, and the arm",
  "compares within one build."
 ],
 "models": [
  { "path": "sss_fixture.gltf" }
 ],
 "materials": {
  "sss_skin_a": { "sss": { "color": [1.00, 0.10, 0.10], "radius": 0.35 } },
  "sss_skin_b": { "sss": { "color": [0.10, 1.00, 0.12], "radius": 0.35 } }
 },
 "lights": [
  {
   "name": "SkinKey",
   "type": "directional",
   "direction": [0.93, -0.26, -0.26],
   "color": [1.0, 0.97, 0.92],
   "intensity": 3.0,
   "cast_shadows": false
  }
 ],
 "camera": {
  "eye": [0.0, 1.0, 20.6],
  "target": [0.0, 1.0, 0.0],
  "fov": 40
 },
 "post": {
  "tonemap": "neutral",
  "exposure": 1.0
 }
}
